<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Act of Security 1704</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1704">1704</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Act of Security</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Act of Security 1704 was a law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland">Parliament of Scotland</a> that reserved its right to choose a different successor to Queen Anne from the one chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, unless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> received guarantees of its religion, freedom and trade. It also allowed the arming of the Scottish militia. England answered with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alien-act-1705">Alien Act</a> of 1705, and the dispute led to the Union of 1707.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/act-of-security-1704.jpg" alt="Act of Security 1704" width="320" height="310" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Scotland_(1660-1689).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Queen Anne's last surviving child, William, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a>, died in 1700, and both the English and the Scottish parliaments had to find a Protestant successor. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement 1701</a> the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> settled the crown on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-of-hanover">Electress Sophia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, a granddaughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a>, without consulting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland">Parliament of Scotland</a>. The Scottish Parliament answered in 1703 with a bill of its own, but the Lord High Commissioner to the Parliament of Scotland refused it royal assent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The measure, also called the Act for the Security of the Kingdom, provided that if Anne died without issue the three Estates of the Scottish Parliament would appoint a Protestant successor from among the descendants of the Scottish kings. They were not to choose the English successor unless various economic, political and religious conditions were met. The bill became an act in 1704, after the Scottish Parliament refused to raise taxes, and sought to withdraw its troops from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-churchill-1st-duke-of-marlborough">Duke of Marlborough</a>'s army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, unless royal assent was given.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The English Parliament retaliated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alien-act-1705">Alien Act 1705</a>, which threatened to cut trade and free movement between the two countries unless negotiations opened leading either to the repeal of the Act of Security or to an Act of Union. The second course was taken, and in 1707 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> were united as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a>, about a hundred years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-the-crowns">Union of the Crowns</a>. The new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-great-britain">Parliament of Great Britain</a> then explicitly repealed the act, together with the Peace and War Act 1703, by the Repeal of Certain Scotch Acts 1707.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Act of Security 1704” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Union became urgent after the English Act of Settlement of 1701 fixed the succession on the Protestant House of Hanover, while the Scottish Parliament’s <b>Act of Security</b> of 1704 reserved the right to choose a different successor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
